{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>For they are adversaries to you, etc.</b> In the preceding verse there appears to be a redundancy of expression: ״צָרוֹר אֶת הַמִּדְיָנִים וְהִכִּיתֶם אוֹתָם״ — what is the difference between 'harass' (צָרוֹר) and 'and you shall smite them' (וְהִכִּיתֶם)? And our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Tanhuma Pinchas 3): from here — if one comes to kill you, rise early to kill him. It should have said 'to kill him' (לְהָרְגוֹ), since the Midianites started the provocation. And the expression 'they are adversaries' (צוֹרְרִים) requires explanation, for its meaning is that they are still adversaries to you. And perhaps HaShem knew what was in the hearts of the Midianites — that their rage had not yet subsided and they are still adversaries, devising schemes to devise plots in wickedness, and perhaps more than at first, because the fury over their sister burned in their hearts to take vengeance; therefore, rise early to harass them. And according to its plain sense, 'adversaries' (צוֹרְרִים) is a noun, to say that from of old they have been your adversaries: for in the beginning, 'וְהַמְּדָנִים מָכְרוּ אוֹתוֹ לְפוֹטִיפַר' (Genesis 37:36); afterward, Midian made peace with Moab against Israel; afterward, they abandoned their daughters to cause Israel to stumble. Thus it is found that the Midianites have from of old been a continuous chain of rebellion against Israel. Therefore He said that they are adversaries to you." "And in order to settle the redundancy, I say that HaShem commanded two things. One: 'Harass the Midianites' — to afflict them first for many days, and afterward 'and you shall smite them' — and all this, why? Because they are adversaries to you. And this is giving a reason for 'harass' (צָרוֹר), for they too afflicted you, by means of the harlots who gave you wine to drink; and when she said to him, 'Let me serve,' she would say to him: worship idolatry first. And this is what it says: 'כִּי צוֹרְרִים הֵם לָכֶם בְּנִכְלֵיהֶם אֲשֶׁר נִכְּלוּ עַל דְּבַר פְּעוֹר' — for the whole essence of this affliction that they inflicted upon you was the intention to compel you to Peor. And regarding what He said, 'and you shall smite them,' He gave a reason and said: 'עַל דְּבַר כָּזְבִּי בַת נְשִׂיא מִדְיָן אֲחֹתָם הַמֻּכָּה בְּיוֹם הַמַּגֵּפָה עַל דְּבַר פְּעוֹר' — because their sister is the slain one, they too will scheme to take vengeance and to strike you. Therefore, rise early to strike them — for if one comes to kill you, rise early to kill him. And perhaps this is why it says 'וַיְהִי אַחֲרֵי הַמַּגֵּפָה' at the end of the verse and with an etnahta — to expound it both as what follows after and what precedes: after it, according to its plain sense; and before it, to say that even after the plague they want to strike you on account of their slain sister — therefore you shall rise early to strike them."], :book :numbers, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:43:11.961406917Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}